{#-
  Lido — mint wstETH from ETH, and optionally exit through Lido's withdrawal
  queue.

  The mint leg scopes the one-way conversion WETH → ETH → stETH → wstETH. This
  is the collateral-acquisition leg of a leveraged wstETH/WETH position:
  `submit` mints stETH at Lido's exchange rate with no market involved, so the
  leg carries no slippage and needs no price checker.

  The exit leg (`exit: true`) scopes the protocol's own redemption path:
  wstETH → withdrawal queue → ETH → WETH. It is the alternative to selling the
  collateral on a swap venue — no slippage and no price checker either, paid at
  Lido's rate, in exchange for a multi-day wait.

  Required template params:
    (none)

  Optional params:
    exit         — bool, default false. `false` scopes the mint leg only, which
                   is what this template has always done. `true` additionally
                   scopes the wstETH approve to the queue,
                   `requestWithdrawalsWstETH`, `claimWithdrawals` and
                   `WETH.deposit`.
    max_approval — exclusive upper bound (in wei) for the stETH `approve`
                   amount, emitted as `less_than max_approval`. Defaults to
                   uint256.max, which only rules out the literal
                   infinite-approval sentinel and does NOT bound exposure.
                   Set it to a realistic operational ceiling.
    max_exit_approval — the same, for the wstETH `approve` to the withdrawal
                   queue. Read only when `exit` is true; mint-only configs need
                   not declare it.

  Resolved from aliases (NOT params):
    aliases.tokens.WETH           — WETH, unwrapped to ETH and re-wrapped on exit
    aliases.tokens.stETH          — Lido's stETH; also the staking entrypoint
    aliases.tokens.wstETH         — the wstETH wrapper
    aliases.lido.withdrawal_queue — unstETH, read only when `exit` is true
    aliases.ZERO                  — auto-injected, used to pin the referral

  The addresses are fixed by the protocol, so none of them is a param: there is
  nothing for a deployment to choose.

  Policy enforced (mint leg):
    WETH.withdraw(amount)          amount pass
    stETH.submit(referral)         referral == aliases.ZERO,
                                   execution_options: "send"
    stETH.approve(wstETH, amount)  spender pinned to the wrapper,
                                   amount < max_approval
    wstETH.wrap(amount)            amount pass

  Policy enforced (exit leg, `exit: true` only):
    wstETH.approve(queue, amount)  spender pinned to the queue,
                                   amount < max_exit_approval
    queue.requestWithdrawalsWstETH(amounts, owner)
                                   amounts pass, owner == avatar
    queue.claimWithdrawals(ids, hints)
                                   both pass
    WETH.deposit()                 execution_options: "send"

  ── Why `exit` defaults to false rather than being required ──────────────────

  The Morpho and Aave templates make their `borrow` gate REQUIRED, and this one
  deliberately does not. That is a different tradeoff, not a different
  situation. `morpho_blue.tmpl` already existed when #44 added its borrow
  surface, and the required gate landed in the same change (ddc00f1) precisely
  because existing configs pointed at it — the position this template is in now.
  Morpho chose to refuse to render until an author answers, accepting that every
  existing config breaks until it is edited. Here `false` reproduces, call for
  call, the policy this template emitted before the leg existed, so no existing
  config changes on its next apply and none has to be touched.

  What that costs, and it is worth knowing before copying the pattern: a
  defaulted gate records no decision. `exit | default(false)` substitutes a
  value before anything can object, so a misspelled key — `exti`, `Exit`,
  `exit_leg` — silently renders the mint-only policy. `throwOnUndefined` cannot
  catch it: it fires on emitting an undefined value, and the default means there
  never is one. The `borrow` templates get that protection only because
  `bool(undefined)` throws when no default supplies a value first. T11-16 pins
  this behaviour so it stays a known gap rather than a surprise.

  The `# exit:` line below is not the safety net it may look like either. It is
  visible when rendering the template directly, and nowhere downstream:
  `runGenerate` parses the rendered YAML with `toJSON()` and re-emits it through
  `serializeRoleStates`, so no comment survives into the generated artifact.
  The same is true of `# borrow:` in `aave_v3.tmpl` and `morpho_blue.tmpl`.

  ── Things worth knowing before enabling the exit leg ────────────────────────

  A pending withdrawal is an NFT, not a balance. `requestWithdrawalsWstETH`
  burns the wstETH immediately and mints an unstETH ERC-721 to the owner; the
  ETH only appears at `claimWithdrawals`, after an oracle report finalises the
  request. Any valuation that reads token balances will therefore see the
  position disappear at request and reappear at claim, understating NAV by the
  in-flight amount for days. Decide how the NFT is valued BEFORE granting this,
  not after the first request.

  The queue is slowest exactly when it is most needed. Finalisation lengthens
  with the size of the exit queue, so this is a cost-optimisation path — it
  avoids swap fees and slippage on unwinds that can wait — and not a stress
  path. Keep a swap venue scoped for exits that have to close now.

  `_owner` is pinned to the avatar, so the claim NFT is minted to the Safe and
  nowhere else. `claimWithdrawalsTo` takes a `_recipient` and is deliberately
  NOT scoped: `claimWithdrawals` pays the owner, which the previous constraint
  has already pinned, so the pair leaves no way to direct the proceeds off the
  Safe. Adding `claimWithdrawalsTo` later would undo that in one line.

  `requestWithdrawals` (the stETH-denominated variant) is also not scoped. The
  queue accepts wstETH directly, so a position held in wstETH needs no unwrap,
  and leaving both `unwrap` and the stETH variant out keeps the exit path
  single-file: wstETH in, ETH out. A config whose Safe holds raw stETH needs a
  deliberate extension here rather than an unwrap grant.

  The unstETH mint does NOT call `onERC721Received`. Lido implements ERC-721 by
  hand and mints by emitting `Transfer(0, owner, id)` directly; the receiver
  check is only reachable from `safeTransferFrom`. So a Safe whose fallback
  handler has been zeroed — a common hardening step, and one that removes
  `onERC721Received` — can still hold the claim. The converse is worth
  remembering: with no fallback handler, nobody can `safeTransferFrom` an
  unstETH NFT INTO such a Safe, only `transferFrom`.

  ── Value-bearing calls ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  `submit` and, on the exit leg, `WETH.deposit` are the value-bearing calls in
  this repo, hence their `send` execution option. Send permits attaching ETH and
  says nothing about how much. Both amounts are unbounded because of a ZAC gap,
  not a platform limitation: Roles V2 does have an ether allowance, surfaced by
  the pinned SDK as `etherWithinAllowance` in the very options object where zac
  writes `send`, but the DSL has no allowance operator and `executionFlags` maps
  only `send` and `delegatecall`. So the value is bounded by the Safe's ETH
  balance alone. That is the mechanism to reach for if a future policy needs a
  real ceiling — do not conclude from this template that none exists.

  The unbounded amounts are acceptable here because both destinations are pinned
  and both credit `msg.sender`: the Safe converts its own ETH into its own
  stETH, or its own ETH into its own WETH. A compromised role member can change
  the Safe's asset mix but cannot move value to an address it does not control.
  Do not reuse `send` against a target that can forward funds elsewhere without
  re-examining all of this.

  `WETH.withdraw` pays out with a 2300-gas `transfer`, which is not enough for a
  Safe proxy's fallback on a COLD access (a 2100-gas SLOAD of the singleton slot
  plus a 2600-gas cold DELEGATECALL). It works here regardless, because the call
  arrives through the Roles modifier: `execTransactionFromModule` has already
  touched the proxy and the singleton in the same transaction, so both are warm
  and the fallback costs ~200 gas. Do not infer from an isolated failing probe
  that WETH cannot unwrap to a Safe — it cannot only when nothing has touched
  the Safe yet in that transaction, which is never the case on this path.

  The referral is pinned to the zero address rather than left to the caller.
  Lido pays referral rewards to whoever is named, so an unpinned parameter would
  let a role member redirect a benefit away from the vault on every mint,
  without ever failing the gate.

  `max_approval` / `max_exit_approval` bound the STANDING allowance, not
  cumulative volume. `approve` overwrites rather than increments and
  `increaseAllowance` is not scoped, so the allowance itself can never exceed
  the ceiling. But `wrap` and `requestWithdrawalsWstETH` take any amount and
  nothing rate-limits the approve-then-act loop, so total volume is unbounded
  whatever the ceiling is set to. Bounding the loop would need
  `callWithinAllowance`, which the DSL cannot express either. If
  `increaseAllowance` is ever added here, the ceilings become bypassable by
  repeated increments.

  One rendering note: `withdraw`, `wrap` and the withdrawal-queue array params
  have `pass`, so the plan emits those calls as selector-only (wildcarded)
  grants. Their `params:` blocks constrain nothing beyond the selector — which
  is what `pass` means, but it reads like a constraint.
-#}
{#- Assert `exit` is a real boolean before anything reads it. YAML quoting picks
    the type and nunjucks picks truthiness, and together they fail open: `false` is
    the boolean, but `"false"` is a non-empty string, which is truthy, so a bare
    `{% if exit %}` would open the gate on the value an author wrote to close it. -#}
{%- set exit = exit | default(false) | bool("exit") -%}
# exit: {{ exit }}
roles:
  - address: "{{ aliases.tokens.WETH }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function withdraw(uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
{%- if exit %}

      - signature: "function deposit()"
        execution_options: "send"
{%- endif %}

  - address: "{{ aliases.tokens.stETH }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function submit(address referral)"
        execution_options: "send"
        params:
          - name: referral
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to"
            value: "{{ aliases.ZERO }}"
            value_type: "address"

      - signature: "function approve(address spender, uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: spender
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to"
            value: "{{ aliases.tokens.wstETH }}"
            value_type: "address"
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "less_than"
            value: "{{ max_approval | default('115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129639935') }}"
            value_type: "uint256"

  - address: "{{ aliases.tokens.wstETH }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function wrap(uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "pass"
{%- if exit %}

      - signature: "function approve(address spender, uint256 amount)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: spender
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to"
            value: "{{ aliases.lido.withdrawal_queue }}"
            value_type: "address"
          - name: amount
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "less_than"
            value: "{{ max_exit_approval | default('115792089237316195423570985008687907853269984665640564039457584007913129639935') }}"
            value_type: "uint256"

  - address: "{{ aliases.lido.withdrawal_queue }}"
    functions:
      - signature: "function requestWithdrawalsWstETH(uint256[] _amounts, address _owner)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: _amounts
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: _owner
            param_type: "static"
            operator: "equal_to_avatar"

      - signature: "function claimWithdrawals(uint256[] _requestIds, uint256[] _hints)"
        execution_options: "none"
        params:
          - name: _requestIds
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
          - name: _hints
            param_type: "dynamic"
            operator: "pass"
{%- endif %}
